# M / B003 — MAP.md, rules angular/api/architecture/coding-style.

C R12 OL FE-001 | DEFAULT | Use modern standalone Angular APIs for new code organized primarily by feature; no NgModules for ordinary feature development unless an integration or legacy dependency genuinely requires them. | Angular source | Integration or legacy dependency.
C R13 OL FE-007 | MUST | Keep the frontend structure feature-oriented: no application-wide technical buckets (global components/, services/, models/) or empty architecture folders; feature code stays in its feature; core and shared stay relatively small. | Angular source | none stated
C R14 OL FE-002 | MUST | Keep pages and components focused on presentation and UI orchestration; do not move trivial presentation logic into services merely to shrink components. | Angular source | none stated
C R15 OL FE-004 | DEFAULT | Use Signals for naturally synchronous local UI state and RxJS when asynchronous streams, HTTP composition, cancellation or event pipelines make it clearer; avoid mechanical conversions. | Angular source | none stated
C R16 OL FE-006 | DEFAULT | Prefer simple component, signal or feature-service state before introducing a state-management library. | Angular source | none stated
C R17 OL FE-003 | MUST | Keep backend communication in explicit feature data-access services or another explicit HTTP boundary; do not scatter HttpClient calls through presentation components and do not import backend Domain entities into the Angular project (use frontend API models matching the published contract). | Angular source | none stated
C R18 OL FE-008 | DEFAULT | Prefer functional interceptors for new code; keep interceptor responsibilities cross-cutting and never put feature business logic in an interceptor. | Angular source | none stated
C R19 OL FE-009 | MUST | Follow the documented API error contract: centralize reusable ProblemDetails parsing where practical and do not infer business behavior from human-readable exception messages. | Angular source | none stated
C R20 OL FE-005 | DEFAULT | Use route-level lazy loading for meaningful features; do not lazy-load every tiny component. | Angular source | none stated
C R21 OL FE-010 | MUST | Use reactive forms for non-trivial forms when useful; frontend validation improves user experience but never replaces backend validation, data-integrity or security enforcement. | Angular source | none stated
C R22 OL FE-011 | DEFAULT | Avoid manual nested subscriptions; prefer composition operators when they express the behavior clearly. | Angular source | none stated
C R23 OL FE-012 | MUST | When manually subscribing from framework-managed code, tie the subscription lifetime to the component or service lifecycle using current Angular facilities. | Angular source | none stated
C R24 OL API-001 | MUST | HTTP status codes and transport response semantics belong to the API layer; Application and Domain must not return IActionResult or ASP.NET Core status codes. | backend C# | none stated
C R25 OL API-003 | MUST | Choose 200/201/202/204 by actual HTTP semantics (not 200 for every outcome, not 201 merely because a row was inserted, not 202 for synchronously completed work; 204 has no body); keep status-code behavior consistent across similar endpoints. | backend C# | none stated
C R26 OL API-004 | DEFAULT | A successful collection query with zero matches returns 200 with an empty collection, not 404. | backend C# | none stated
C R27 OL API-005 | MUST | 401 when authentication is missing or failed, 403 when an authenticated principal is not permitted; returning 404 to hide a protected resource must be a deliberate, consistent security policy. | backend C# | Deliberate consistent 404 policy.
C R28 OL API-006 | MUST | 409 for a valid request conflicting with current state; 412 when an explicit HTTP precondition such as If-Match fails. | backend C# | none stated
C R29 OL API-013 | MUST NOT | Do not introduce API versioning merely because an API exists; add it when published compatibility or coexistence of client generations creates a concrete need. | backend C# | none stated
C R30 OL API-014 | MUST | Once external clients rely on an API version avoid silent breaking changes: a breaking contract change requires by default a new version or another explicitly approved compatibility strategy; compilation alone does not establish compatibility. | published APIs | Explicitly approved compatibility strategy.
C R31 OL API-015 | DEFAULT | When explicit versioning is required use one strategy consistently; URL-segment versioning by default unless requirements justify header or query-string versioning. | versioned APIs | Justified header or query-string versioning.
C R32 OL API-016 | MUST | Swagger/OpenAPI metadata describes the API that actually runs (routes, important response statuses including binding, validation, authentication/authorization, rate limiting and centralized exception handling, schemas, authentication behavior); no speculative responses. | API documentation | none stated
C R33 OL API-017 | DEFAULT | Enable Swagger UI in Development by default; expose it in other environments only when deployment requirements explicitly justify it. | API documentation | Deployment requirements.
C R34 OL API-018 | MUST | Health checks stay cheap and deterministic and do not become full business workflows or perform expensive or mutating database work. | health endpoints | none stated
C R35 OL API-019 | MUST | Liveness and readiness semantics stay distinct: liveness by default avoids external dependencies that could cause restart loops; readiness may depend on required capabilities and stays unhealthy until required startup work completes (never liveness for that). | health endpoints | none stated
C R36 OL API-020 | MUST NOT | Publicly reachable health responses must not expose secrets, credentials, connection details, stack traces or unnecessary internal diagnostics. | health endpoints | none stated
C R37 OL ARC-001 | MUST | Dependencies point inward: Domain depends on no application project, Application on Domain, Infrastructure on Application and Domain, Api on Application and Infrastructure only for composition; never Domain -> Application/Infrastructure or Application -> Infrastructure; reconsider the abstraction instead of adding an outward reference. | backend projects | none stated
C R38 OL ARC-028 | MUST NOT | Backend test projects get no broader production-project references than their scope requires (Domain.Tests -> Domain; Application.Tests -> Application and Domain as needed; IntegrationTests -> Api; Infrastructure.Tests -> Infrastructure, narrower as required). | backend test projects | none stated
C R39 OL ARC-002 | MUST | Keep Domain free of EF Core, ASP.NET Core, HTTP, persistence and external-service implementation concerns. | backend Domain | none stated
C R40 OL ARC-003 | DEFAULT | Simple domain entities are the default; for CRUD-oriented applications and straightforward workflows enforce use-case and workflow rules in Application Services; validation rules, immutable fields, status transitions or allowed/forbidden edits do not by themselves justify a Rich Domain Model. | backend Domain | none stated
C R41 OL ARC-004 | REQUIRES_APPROVAL | Move behavior into Domain entities only when non-trivial invariants or domain behavior materially benefit from domain-level encapsulation independently of a use case; otherwise a Rich Domain Model or DDD tactical pattern requires explicit approval unless the approved specification requires it. | backend Domain | Approved specification requires the design.
C R42 OL ARC-005 | MUST NOT | Do not add factory methods, private setters, transition methods, Aggregate Roots, Domain Events, Specifications, Domain Services, systematic Value Objects or similar DDD constructs merely to make a simple model appear domain-driven. | backend Domain | none stated
C R43 OL ARC-006 | DEFAULT | Application Services are the default orchestration mechanism for use cases; a service becoming too large is a signal to reconsider responsibilities, not to introduce another architectural pattern. | backend Application | none stated
C R44 OL ARC-007 | MUST | Define persistence and external-service abstractions in Application when use cases require them; keep infrastructure implementation details out of Application. | backend Application | none stated
C R45 OL ARC-008 | MUST NOT | Application must not use DbContext, DbSet<T>, EF Core APIs, provider-specific types or ASP.NET Core transport types. | backend Application | none stated
C R46 OL ARC-010 | DEFAULT | ASP.NET Core Controllers are the default HTTP entry point and stay thin (bind and validate HTTP input, invoke Application services, translate outcomes); no Minimal API endpoint groups unless explicitly requested. | backend Api | Explicit request for Minimal API.
C R47 OL ARC-011 | MUST NOT | Controllers must not contain business logic, access DbContext or concrete repositories, or construct infrastructure services manually. | backend Api | none stated
C R48 OL ARC-012 | DEFAULT | Program.cs is the application composition root and may register Infrastructure implementations; Controllers do not consume Infrastructure implementation types directly. | backend Api | none stated
C R49 OL ARC-013 | DEFAULT | Prefer specific repository interfaces representing cohesive Application persistence needs over table-mirroring generic CRUD contracts; define them in Application and implement them in Infrastructure. | repositories | none stated
C R50 OL ARC-014 | MUST NOT | Repository interfaces must not expose IQueryable<T>, DbSet<T>, DbContext, EF Core expressions or provider-specific types; LINQ-to-EF query construction stays in Infrastructure. | repositories | none stated
C R51 OL ARC-015 | MUST NOT | Application must not depend on IGenericRepository<T> or another generic CRUD contract; a generic repository base may exist only as an Infrastructure detail removing genuine duplication and is not expanded into a replacement for EF Core queries. | repositories | none stated
C R52 OL ARC-016 | DEFAULT | IUnitOfWork (SaveChangesAsync) is the default commit boundary: repositories stage changes and do not call SaveChangesAsync independently; Application Services decide when to commit; Infrastructure implements IUnitOfWork with the application's DbContext. | Unit of Work | none stated
C R53 OL ARC-017 | MUST | Repositories in one use case share the same Unit of Work and scoped DbContext; repositories are injected explicitly and IUnitOfWork is not a repository container or service locator. | Unit of Work | none stated
C R54 OL ARC-018 | DEFAULT | Do not introduce explicit transaction APIs until a concrete use case requires transaction control beyond normal SaveChangesAsync. | Unit of Work | Concrete need.
C R55 OL ARC-019 | MUST | EF Core is an Infrastructure implementation detail: use it with its full capabilities inside Infrastructure, do not leak EF Core concepts into Application and do not cripple repository implementations to preserve a generic abstraction. | EF Core usage | none stated
C R56 OL ARC-021 | DEFAULT | Use constructor injection for required dependencies by default; avoid service-locator and property-injection patterns. | dependency injection | none stated
C R57 OL ARC-022 | MUST | Respect dependency lifetimes: never capture a shorter-lived dependency inside a longer-lived service. | dependency injection | none stated
C R58 OL ARC-023 | DEFAULT | Keep registration close to the owning layer: Application registers Application services, Infrastructure persistence and adapters, Api remains the composition root. | dependency injection | none stated
C R59 OL RUN-022 | DEFAULT | Prefer strongly typed options for structured service configuration, validate settings at startup when missing or invalid values prevent correct or secure operation, and avoid scattering string-based IConfiguration lookups through services. | configuration | Composition and registration code.
C R60 OL DEP-021 | DEFAULT | When Serilog is configured use the modern DI-integrated builder.Services.AddSerilog(...) rather than UseSerilog, keep provider configuration in environment-aware configuration and keep application code depending on ILogger<T>. | logging setup | none stated
C R61 OL ARC-024 | MUST | Keep cache technology out of Domain and do not leak infrastructure cache APIs into Application without a deliberate boundary abstraction. | caching | none stated
C R62 OL ARC-025 | MUST | Infrastructure translates remote HTTP responses into Application-facing abstractions; do not leak HttpResponseMessage or remote transport concerns into Application. | outbound HTTP | none stated
C R63 OL ARC-026 | MUST NOT | Do not create separate Domain or persistence models merely because the external API has multiple versions; isolate version-specific transport contracts at the API boundary. | API versions | none stated
C R64 OL ARC-029 | MUST NOT | Do not duplicate equivalent DTOs across Api and Application merely to create layer-shaped types; introduce transport-specific contracts when separation provides a concrete boundary benefit. | transport contracts | none stated
C R65 OL RUN-009 | DEFAULT | Follow the repository's existing conventions and .editorconfig; use modern C# when it improves clarity but do not rewrite working code solely to use newer syntax. | backend C# | none stated
C R66 OL RUN-024 | DEFAULT | For new C# code prefer file-scoped namespaces when consistent, keep one primary top-level type per file with a matching file name, and use the Async suffix for Task/ValueTask methods unless an existing or framework contract makes it inappropriate; do not rename established APIs solely to add the suffix. | new backend C# | Existing or framework contract.
C R67 OL RUN-013 | DEFAULT | Use ILogger<T> with structured message templates and stable property names; avoid string interpolation that turns structured values into opaque text. | logging calls | none stated
C R68 OL RUN-011 | MUST NOT | Do not perform style-only rewrites outside the requested change unless explicitly asked; avoid mixing large formatting or modernization changes with functional work; do not reorganize existing files or convert constructors mechanically. | backend C# | Explicit request.
C X4 EX - | MUST | Ownership of instruction content: rules and CLAUDE.md hold normative policy; workflow skills hold procedures, stop conditions and output shape; knowledge skills hold technique and examples only and own no policy; hooks, scripts and configuration enforce what is mechanically checkable; a pointer or check-by-reference is never a second owner. | instruction-system maintenance | none stated | Instruction-system ownership rule; the oracle does not regulate where instruction content lives. | Adding a binding rule only to a knowledge skill violates the ownership split.
C X5 EX - | MUST | New instruction content is placed by kind (planning-time governance and decisions in the compact CLAUDE.md or technology-stack; area constraints in a flat path rule whose paths cover the needed scope; procedures, stop conditions and output formats in workflow skills; technique in knowledge skills or their references; mechanical checks in scripts or hooks plus a concise rule), after which MAP.md is updated and check-instructions is run. | instruction-system maintenance | none stated | Instruction-system maintenance procedure; no oracle policy governs instruction placement. | Adding a new rule file without updating the map and running the checker does not follow the procedure.
C X6 EX - | MUST (checker-enforced) | Hooks are registered in exec form (node plus the script path anchored at ${CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR}, no shell), including the dependency guard for PostToolUseFailure; the PreToolUse guard fails closed (onFailure block) while advisory hooks fail open. | hook configuration | none stated | Hook wiring is template infrastructure; no oracle policy addresses hook registration or failure modes. | A shell-form hook command fails the instruction-structure check.
C X7 EX API-013,ARC-020 | REQUIRES_APPROVAL (Reserved) | Adopting API versioning is a Reserved decision even when a concrete compatibility need exists. | HTTP API versioning | none stated | API-013 allows introducing versioning for a concrete compatibility need without requiring approval; ARC-020's consequential-change list does not name versioning adoption. | With published clients needing coexistence the agent must still raise a Decision Request before adding versioning.

